==== Wireless (Brume-W Only) ====

Wireless may work after installing the following additional packages:
  * [[:packages:pkgdata:kmod-rtl8xxxu]]
  * [[:packages:pkgdata:rtl8192eu-firmware]]

There have been reports of the wireless not working ( https://www.reddit.com/r/openwrt/comments/muqwjc/glinet_mv1000w_wifi_modules/ ), so your results may vary.

====== uBoot method ======
  - Download one of the ''glinet_gl-mv1000'' OpenWRT images (21+ or snapshot).  Choose [[:docs:techref:image.format:|either the ''ext4'' or ''squashfs'' filesystem]].
    *  The images are called "sdcard", but this refers to the internal eMMC card; you don't need anything on the MicroSD slot.
  - ''gunzip'' the image (don't mind "trailing garbage ignored" errors).
  - Configure your PC’s NIC for 192.168.1.2/24, e.g. ''ip addr add dev $yournic 192.168.1.2/24; ip link set $yournic up''. If you’re already in an ''192.168.1.x'' network, you’ll need to disconnect from it first.
  - Plug your PC's NIC to one of Brume’s LAN ports (the WAN port has to be unplugged).
  - The Brume’s reset button, labelled '↻', is to the right of the "Mode" switch. Hold it firm and plug on the USB power.
  - Wait a couple seconds. All three LEDs will turn on, then WAN and VPN will turn off, then WAN back on. Release the button at this point. You should now have both Power and WAN steady on. Verify that you can ping 192.168.1.1.
  - On a browser, go to http://192.168.1.1 (not ''https:'') and upload the image.
  - The upload webpage will hang there, but the device will reboot automatically. You’ll know it worked when you can ''ssh root@192.168.1.1'' (no password by default, set one).
  - The root partition will be small; if you want to use the rest of the eMMC, partition ''/dev/mmcblk1'' (see below on "Basic Coonfiguration").

